    ($SATQ$8f6d6ae6$SATQ$, $SATQ$87c679a7-fd47-4d88-a4f7-43658338277e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$3619829b-7bac-4124-bab7-68121f7e67ca$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Archaeologists have estimated that the pre-Columbian Native American city of Cahokia, located across the Mississippi River from modern-day St. Louis, Missouri, had as many as 20,000 inhabitants in the year 1150 <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> it one of the largest cities in North America at the time.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"445203ad-0e67-4db8-8938-b617bfe4ad8a","content":"<p>CE making</p>"},{"id":"30bd25bb-f444-4cbc-b353-4a8c5acb8bad","content":"<p>CE. Making</p>"},{"id":"d8a5fb15-e52e-401f-acdc-4ed1e5fe5eb0","content":"<p>CE, making</p>"},{"id":"665fae40-59b8-4beb-b6b1-aebc2d6ba43a","content":"<p>CE; making</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d8a5fb15-e52e-401f-acdc-4ed1e5fe5eb0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The phrase &ldquo;making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; provides additional information about Cahokia that&rsquo;s not required for the sentence to make sense or function grammatically. As a nonessential supplement, this phrase should be separated from the rest of the sentence with a comma. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence. The nonessential descriptive aside &ldquo;making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; needs to be separated from the rest of the sentence with a comma. Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a sentence fragment. &ldquo;Making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t have a subject and can&rsquo;t stand on its own as a sentence. Thus, it can&rsquo;t be separated from the rest of the sentence with a period. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. &ldquo;Making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t have a subject and can&rsquo;t stand on its own as an independent clause. Since a semicolon can only link two independent clauses, using one here creates an error. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-M008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d79b38f2-9b3c-42b7-bef5-3e7c0f5d1df4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"8f6d6ae6","external_id":"87c679a7-fd47-4d88-a4f7-43658338277e","vaultid":"3619829b-7bac-4124-bab7-68121f7e67ca","uId":"52794d81-491d-4593-b751-164bd1afb441","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Archaeologists have estimated that the pre-Columbian Native American city of Cahokia, located across the Mississippi River from modern-day St. Louis, Missouri, had as many as 20,000 inhabitants in the year 1150 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> it one of the largest cities in North America at the time.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"445203ad-0e67-4db8-8938-b617bfe4ad8a","content":"<p>CE making</p>"},{"id":"30bd25bb-f444-4cbc-b353-4a8c5acb8bad","content":"<p>CE. Making</p>"},{"id":"d8a5fb15-e52e-401f-acdc-4ed1e5fe5eb0","content":"<p>CE, making</p>"},{"id":"665fae40-59b8-4beb-b6b1-aebc2d6ba43a","content":"<p>CE; making</p>"}],"keys":["d8a5fb15-e52e-401f-acdc-4ed1e5fe5eb0"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The phrase &ldquo;making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; provides additional information about Cahokia that&rsquo;s not required for the sentence to make sense or function grammatically. As a nonessential supplement, this phrase should be separated from the rest of the sentence with a comma. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice results in a run-on sentence. The nonessential descriptive aside &ldquo;making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; needs to be separated from the rest of the sentence with a comma. Choice B is incorrect. This choice results in a sentence fragment. &ldquo;Making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t have a subject and can&rsquo;t stand on its own as a sentence. Thus, it can&rsquo;t be separated from the rest of the sentence with a period. Choice D is incorrect. This choice results in a punctuation error. &ldquo;Making&hellip;at the time&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t have a subject and can&rsquo;t stand on its own as an independent clause. Since a semicolon can only link two independent clauses, using one here creates an error. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-M008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d79b38f2-9b3c-42b7-bef5-3e7c0f5d1df4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$908a76b8$SATQ$, $SATQ$bbade20e-2e78-447d-b72f-da4a848bb096$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>When a given industry&mdash;water and electricity are two well-known examples&mdash;carries high infrastructural start-up costs and other barriers that discourage competition, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> of just one or two suppliers per municipality. Such industries are known as natural monopolies.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"65cb3266-7ed3-4a5c-b5db-c796a8b6a1d5","content":"<p>these often consist</p>"},{"id":"c1f5a5c3-dd43-498e-a9be-02c131109eec","content":"<p>they often consist</p>"},{"id":"8085ef2e-aafc-431d-b8e1-72c11cb42259","content":"<p>it often consists</p>"},{"id":"8ee5a7a3-620c-4cc5-a562-1862f4c9deb4","content":"<p>this often consists</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8085ef2e-aafc-431d-b8e1-72c11cb42259"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun "it" agrees in number with the singular antecedent "industry" and clearly indicates that the industry consists of just one or two suppliers per municipality.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The plural pronoun "these" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent "industry" nor clearly indicates that the industry&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as "start-up costs" or "barriers"&mdash;consists of just one or two suppliers per municipality. Choice B is incorrect because the plural pronoun "they" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent "industry." Choice D is incorrect because the singular pronoun "this" is ambiguous in this context; the resulting sentence leaves unclear what consists of just one or two suppliers per municipality.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"908a76b8","external_id":"bbade20e-2e78-447d-b72f-da4a848bb096","vaultid":null,"uId":"0c63a8d3-f463-4ab8-a0ac-d1b40c05a05d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>When a given industry&mdash;water and electricity are two well-known examples&mdash;carries high infrastructural start-up costs and other barriers that discourage competition, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> of just one or two suppliers per municipality. Such industries are known as natural monopolies.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"65cb3266-7ed3-4a5c-b5db-c796a8b6a1d5","content":"<p>these often consist</p>"},{"id":"c1f5a5c3-dd43-498e-a9be-02c131109eec","content":"<p>they often consist</p>"},{"id":"8085ef2e-aafc-431d-b8e1-72c11cb42259","content":"<p>it often consists</p>"},{"id":"8ee5a7a3-620c-4cc5-a562-1862f4c9deb4","content":"<p>this often consists</p>"}],"keys":["8085ef2e-aafc-431d-b8e1-72c11cb42259"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is pronoun-antecedent agreement. The singular pronoun \"it\" agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"industry\" and clearly indicates that the industry consists of just one or two suppliers per municipality.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The plural pronoun \"these\" neither agrees in number with the singular antecedent \"industry\" nor clearly indicates that the industry&mdash;not another plural noun in the sentence, such as \"start-up costs\" or \"barriers\"&mdash;consists of just one or two suppliers per municipality. Choice B is incorrect because the plural pronoun \"they\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular antecedent \"industry.\" Choice D is incorrect because the singular pronoun \"this\" is ambiguous in this context; the resulting sentence leaves unclear what consists of just one or two suppliers per municipality.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$9091458d$SATQ$, $SATQ$bd7408af-a2d1-4c48-8447-ef7d4ca6096c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$a0a400c3-87dd-4f2b-9187-f3aab5e8bb8b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Emperor penguins don&rsquo;t waddle out of the ocean. They launch themselves at such a high speed that they travel up to two meters before landing. How <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> A layer of microbubbles on their plumage reduces friction as the penguins speed to the surface.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"88ad9603-e0b8-4f57-b2a5-24f8ca169dbf","content":"<p>they are able to move so fast!</p>"},{"id":"f7ac3571-efdb-490f-8a9b-3e6895fef516","content":"<p>&nbsp;are they able to move so fast.</p>"},{"id":"409ffffc-6b3f-4e29-a16a-95d707d306a2","content":"<p>they are able to move so fast.</p>"},{"id":"74851c5c-2396-4c65-afea-dfb9700bf4f4","content":"<p>are they able to move so fast?</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["74851c5c-2396-4c65-afea-dfb9700bf4f4"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a question mark to punctuate the interrogative sentence &ldquo;how are they able to move so fast?&rdquo; The interrogative sentence asks a direct question, and the next sentence answers it. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the context requires an interrogative sentence. The exclamative sentence &ldquo;how they are able to move so fast!&rdquo; emphasizes the penguin&rsquo;s high rate of speed, but it doesn&rsquo;t set up the next sentence&rsquo;s explanation of how the penguins achieve such speeds. Choice B is incorrect because a period can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate an interrogative sentence. Choice C is incorrect because the context requires an interrogative sentence. The exclamative sentence &ldquo;how they are able to move so fast&rdquo; emphasizes the penguin&rsquo;s high rate of speed, but it doesn&rsquo;t set up the next sentence&rsquo;s explanation of how the penguins achieve such speeds.
