rdquo; emphasizes the penguin&rsquo;s high rate of speed, but it doesn&rsquo;t set up the next sentence&rsquo;s explanation of how the penguins achieve such speeds. Choice B is incorrect because a period can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate an interrogative sentence. Choice C is incorrect because the context requires an interrogative sentence. The exclamative sentence &ldquo;how they are able to move so fast&rdquo; emphasizes the penguin&rsquo;s high rate of speed, but it doesn&rsquo;t set up the next sentence&rsquo;s explanation of how the penguins achieve such speeds.\n&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-773-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"eb450723-ee02-448c-b4ae-8656319e1c23","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959352,"updateDate":1691007959352}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959352, 1691007959352),
    ($SATQ$91fbd59d$SATQ$, $SATQ$3494d514-155b-4e3a-b3e7-bf6a257b3295$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5dcd8938-e246-4f0c-8e66-4e2eb9f5d3d5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Award-winning cinematographer James Wong Howe was known for his innovative filming techniques. While filming a boxing match for the movie <em>Body and Soul</em> <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Howe had a handheld camera operator wear roller skates. This allowed the operator to move smoothly around actors in a boxing ring, creating an immersive experience for viewers.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"88d277fc-7d36-4e85-90a5-e8535c208ec8","content":"<p>(1947), and</p>"},{"id":"c5a1dcb7-ea44-40f2-b513-849461315a84","content":"<p>(1947),</p>"},{"id":"bbb3a92d-a4a5-47cb-951d-d32efe73e18f","content":"<p>(1947) and</p>"},{"id":"2a2cb1e7-cf09-407b-8779-b3ef793bb963","content":"<p>(1947)</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c5a1dcb7-ea44-40f2-b513-849461315a84"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a supplementary element and a main clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the supplementary phrase (&ldquo;While...1947&rdquo;), which indicates when the action occurred, and the main clause (&ldquo;Howe had a handheld camera operator wear roller skates&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a comma paired with the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between the supplementary element (&ldquo;While&hellip;1947&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;Howe&hellip;skates&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the supplementary element (&ldquo;While&hellip;1947&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;Howe&hellip;skates&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the supplementary element and the main clause with appropriate punctuation. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-778-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e76a013a-4613-463b-beea-cf1657131d68$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"91fbd59d","external_id":"3494d514-155b-4e3a-b3e7-bf6a257b3295","vaultid":"5dcd8938-e246-4f0c-8e66-4e2eb9f5d3d5","uId":"b3c7dccd-14ee-4bee-a2ab-4ec8a78fae4c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Award-winning cinematographer James Wong Howe was known for his innovative filming techniques. While filming a boxing match for the movie <em>Body and Soul</em> <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Howe had a handheld camera operator wear roller skates. This allowed the operator to move smoothly around actors in a boxing ring, creating an immersive experience for viewers.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"88d277fc-7d36-4e85-90a5-e8535c208ec8","content":"<p>(1947), and</p>"},{"id":"c5a1dcb7-ea44-40f2-b513-849461315a84","content":"<p>(1947),</p>"},{"id":"bbb3a92d-a4a5-47cb-951d-d32efe73e18f","content":"<p>(1947) and</p>"},{"id":"2a2cb1e7-cf09-407b-8779-b3ef793bb963","content":"<p>(1947)</p>"}],"keys":["c5a1dcb7-ea44-40f2-b513-849461315a84"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a supplementary element and a main clause. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the supplementary phrase (&ldquo;While...1947&rdquo;), which indicates when the action occurred, and the main clause (&ldquo;Howe had a handheld camera operator wear roller skates&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a comma paired with the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between the supplementary element (&ldquo;While&hellip;1947&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;Howe&hellip;skates&rdquo;). Choice C is incorrect because the conjunction &ldquo;and&rdquo; can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the supplementary element (&ldquo;While&hellip;1947&rdquo;) and the main clause (&ldquo;Howe&hellip;skates&rdquo;). Choice D is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the supplementary element and the main clause with appropriate punctuation. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-778-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e76a013a-4613-463b-beea-cf1657131d68","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959352,"updateDate":1691007959352}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959352, 1691007959352),
    ($SATQ$940ff6f7$SATQ$, $SATQ$e7084bbb-2fb4-4871-be43-276160350da2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Jamaican British artist Willard Wigan is known for his remarkable <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> so small that they are best viewed through a microscope, Wigan&rsquo;s sculptures are made from tiny natural materials, such as spiderweb strands.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7670b92d-e084-4b73-a490-e694c233b820","content":"<p>microsculptures creations</p>"},{"id":"bc84810a-a656-4980-8ade-cae3d6fa3d01","content":"<p>microsculptures, creations</p>"},{"id":"2aa47063-52ec-455f-b10e-aff278791d41","content":"<p>microsculptures. Creations</p>"},{"id":"a3f36842-c70a-4668-9fc0-3e808653ba84","content":"<p>microsculptures and creations</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2aa47063-52ec-455f-b10e-aff278791d41"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between sentences. In this choice, the period is used to correctly mark the boundary between one sentence ("Jamaican&hellip;microsculptures") and another ("Creations&hellip;strands"). The noun phrase beginning with "creations" modifies the subject of the next sentence, "Wigan&rsquo;s sculptures."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences ("Jamaican&hellip;microsculptures" and "Creations&hellip;strands") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice D is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction "and" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"940ff6f7","external_id":"e7084bbb-2fb4-4871-be43-276160350da2","vaultid":null,"uId":"0f12e7c0-7fc4-4fac-a808-1e00730eed14","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Jamaican British artist Willard Wigan is known for his remarkable <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> so small that they are best viewed through a microscope, Wigan&rsquo;s sculptures are made from tiny natural materials, such as spiderweb strands.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7670b92d-e084-4b73-a490-e694c233b820","content":"<p>microsculptures creations</p>"},{"id":"bc84810a-a656-4980-8ade-cae3d6fa3d01","content":"<p>microsculptures, creations</p>"},{"id":"2aa47063-52ec-455f-b10e-aff278791d41","content":"<p>microsculptures. Creations</p>"},{"id":"a3f36842-c70a-4668-9fc0-3e808653ba84","content":"<p>microsculptures and creations</p>"}],"keys":["2aa47063-52ec-455f-b10e-aff278791d41"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between sentences. In this choice, the period is used to correctly mark the boundary between one sentence (\"Jamaican&hellip;microsculptures\") and another (\"Creations&hellip;strands\"). The noun phrase beginning with \"creations\" modifies the subject of the next sentence, \"Wigan&rsquo;s sculptures.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (\"Jamaican&hellip;microsculptures\" and \"Creations&hellip;strands\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice D is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction \"and\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$94de423a$SATQ$, $SATQ$eae18f5c-7abf-4e68-8c7f-315c560a834a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a rural area along the border between Oklahoma and Texas, amateur astronomers gather each year to observe the night sky at the Okie-Tex Star Party. Like most star parties, Okie-Tex takes place in an area with low light pollution, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> dark skies and ideal stargazing conditions.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9d6b798d-12b6-44ae-9886-71dd8d19c0cc","content":"<p>ensuring</p>"},{"id":"283d0ea1-08bc-4b19-b619-d1e3f394f950","content":"<p>this will ensure</p>"},{"id":"ce0b520b-4861-45fe-bdc0-f2364abbc6be","content":"<p>ensures</p>"},{"id":"8ee544f9-43a3-4967-8067-017caf291793","content":"<p>it ensures</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9d6b798d-12b6-44ae-9886-71dd8d19c0cc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite present participle "ensuring" is correctly used to form a supplementary element that modifies the main clause "Okie-Tex takes place in an area with low light pollution," describing the viewing conditions of the star party.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The pronoun "this" and the finite future tense verb "will ensure" would add an additional main clause to this sentence, and the comma after "pollution" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb "ensures" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element to modify the main clause. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The pronoun "it" and the finite present tense verb "ensures" would add an additional main clause to this sentence, and the comma after "pollution" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"94de423a","external_id":"eae18f5c-7abf-4e68-8c7f-315c560a834a","vaultid":null,"uId":"c1180fd1-8888-4778-860e-463d8a60eb13","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a rural area along the border between Oklahoma and Texas, amateur astronomers gather each year to observe the night sky at the Okie-Tex Star Party. Like most star parties, Okie-Tex takes place in an area with low light pollution, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> dark skies and ideal stargazing conditions.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9d6b798d-12b6-44ae-9886-71dd8d19c0cc","content":"<p>ensuring</p>"},{"id":"283d0ea1-08bc-4b19-b619-d1e3f394f950","content":"<p>this will ensure</p>"},{"id":"ce0b520b-4861-45fe-bdc0-f2364abbc6be","content":"<p>ensures</p>"},{"id":"8ee544f9-43a3-4967-8067-017caf291793","content":"<p>it ensures</p>"}],"keys":["9d6b798d-12b6-44ae-9886-71dd8d19c0cc"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite present participle \"ensuring\" is correctly used to form a supplementary element that modifies the main clause \"Okie-Tex takes place in an area with low light pollution,\" describing the viewing conditions of the star party.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The pronoun \"this\" and the finite future tense verb \"will ensure\" would add an additional main clause to this sentence, and the comma after \"pollution\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb \"ensures\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element to modify the main clause. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. The pronoun \"it\" and the finite present tense verb \"ensures\" would add an additional main clause to this sentence, and the comma after \"pollution\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$9579581e$SATQ$, $SATQ$f6f803fa-d374-4157-9aaf-f64022a12a4b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>As cheesemaking practices spread throughout Europe and Asia during and after the Neolithic, divergent strategies for preserving milk <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> whereas rennet-coagulated cheesemaking became key to milk preservation in Europe and Southwest Asia, acid-heat coagulation methods became common among nomadic herding populations of the northeastern Eurasian steppe.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1e1423ee-b215-47a9-9390-fa75fce1dffd","content":"<p>emerged</p>"},{"id":"9fc9f0bb-402b-4597-bb89-6071697eb6f1","content":"<p>emerged and</p>"},{"id":"bb71d325-23f6-4d1c-8881-70b28ea729ae","content":"<p>emerged<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"006171c0-5d64-4266-8341-249b41048512","content":"<p>emerged<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bb71d325-23f6-4d1c-8881-70b28ea729ae"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use within a sentence. A colon can be used between two main clauses to signal that what follows is an elaboration of what came before. In this choice, the colon correctly introduces the following explanation of the divergent milk preservation strategies that emerged.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The main clause ("As&hellip;emerged") and the subordinate clause followed by another main clause ("whereas&hellip;steppe") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction "and" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause ("As&hellip;emerged") and a subordinate clause followed by another main clause ("whereas&hellip;steppe"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause ("As&hellip;emerged") and a subordinate clause followed by another main clause ("whereas&hellip;steppe").</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"9579581e","external_id":"f6f803fa-d374-4157-9aaf-f64022a12a4b","vaultid":null,"uId":"57d7590c-95d3-4689-8283-099d5f223e65","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>As cheesemaking practices spread throughout Europe and Asia during and after the Neolithic, divergent strategies for preserving milk <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> whereas rennet-coagulated cheesemaking became key to milk preservation in Europe and Southwest Asia, acid-heat coagulation methods became common among nomadic herding populations of the northeastern Eurasian steppe.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1e1423ee-b215-47a9-9390-fa75fce1dffd","content":"<p>emerged</p>"},{"id":"9fc9f0bb-402b-4597-bb89-6071697eb6f1","content":"<p>emerged and</p>"},{"id":"bb71d325-23f6-4d1c-8881-70b28ea729ae","content":"<p>emerged<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">:</span></p>"},{"id":"006171c0-5d64-4266-8341-249b41048512","content":"<p>emerged<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span></p>"}],"keys":["bb71d325-23f6-4d1c-8881-70b28ea729ae"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use within a sentence. A colon can be used between two main clauses to signal that what follows is an elaboration of what came before. In this choice, the colon correctly introduces the following explanation of the divergent milk preservation strategies that emerged.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The main clause (\"As&hellip;emerged\") and the subordinate clause followed by another main clause (\"whereas&hellip;steppe\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction \"and\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause (\"As&hellip;emerged\") and a subordinate clause followed by another main clause (\"whereas&hellip;steppe\"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause (\"As&hellip;emerged\") and a subordinate clause followed by another main clause (\"whereas&hellip;steppe\").</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$960dec02$SATQ$, $SATQ$5c126f26-375f-4a6c-8e1b-51954164e3ac$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5f5a815b-638f-47d3-9b79-dbd43d8fcf82$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A recent study tracked the number of bee species present in twenty-seven New York apple orchards over a ten-year period. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> found that when wild growth near an orchard was cleared, the number of different bee species visiting the orchard decreased.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d89d4832-9091-4c59-ab6c-8ef088054064","content":"<p>Entomologist Heather Grab:</p>"},{"id":"cac09006-c7b9-4bdd-b099-1afb9216b772","content":"<p>Entomologist, Heather Grab,</p>"},{"id":"edd62475-9a26-4077-ade4-980e38b7f691","content":"<p>Entomologist Heather Grab</p>"},{"id":"a128dfca-e1cd-4f55-b8ea-e22e5833855f","content":"<p>Entomologist Heather Grab,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["edd62475-9a26-4077-ade4-980e38b7f691"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a name and title and between a subject and a verb. No punctuation is needed between the proper noun &ldquo;Heather Grab&rdquo; and &ldquo;entomologist,&rdquo; the title that describes Grab. Additionally, no punctuation is needed between the sentence&rsquo;s subject (&ldquo;Entomologist Heather Grab&rdquo;) and the main verb (&ldquo;found&rdquo;) that indicates what Grab did. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Setting the entomologist&rsquo;s name off with commas suggests that it could be removed without affecting the coherence of the sentence, which isn&rsquo;t the case. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-714-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$98b65e65-e52e-4d00-9242-faa2a7b4e448$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"960dec02","external_id":"5c126f26-375f-4a6c-8e1b-51954164e3ac","vaultid":"5f5a815b-638f-47d3-9b79-dbd43d8fcf82","uId":"01e245d4-ce4a-48f4-b5f2-6a4dffb23a96","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A recent study tracked the number of bee species present in twenty-seven New York apple orchards over a ten-year period. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> found that when wild growth near an orchard was cleared, the number of different bee species visiting the orchard decreased.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d89d4832-9091-4c59-ab6c-8ef088054064","content":"<p>Entomologist Heather Grab:</p>"},{"id":"cac09006-c7b9-4bdd-b099-1afb9216b772","content":"<p>Entomologist, Heather Grab,</p>"},{"id":"edd62475-9a26-4077-ade4-980e38b7f691","content":"<p>Entomologist Heather Grab</p>"},{"id":"a128dfca-e1cd-4f55-b8ea-e22e5833855f","content":"<p>Entomologist Heather Grab,</p>"}],"keys":["edd62475-9a26-4077-ade4-980e38b7f691"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a name and title and between a subject and a verb. No punctuation is needed between the proper noun &ldquo;Heather Grab&rdquo; and &ldquo;entomologist,&rdquo; the title that describes Grab. Additionally, no punctuation is needed between the sentence&rsquo;s subject (&ldquo;Entomologist Heather Grab&rdquo;) and the main verb (&ldquo;found&rdquo;) that indicates what Grab did. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Setting the entomologist&rsquo;s name off with commas suggests that it could be removed without affecting the coherence of the sentence, which isn&rsquo;t the case. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-714-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"98b65e65-e52e-4d00-9242-faa2a7b4e448","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$96499989$SATQ$, $SATQ$0e57e42b-00aa-455d-8210-140c29759d6e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$899660e0-ee21-4503-93a1-6fce4962bd70$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Fans of science fiction will <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> multiple references to classic sci-fi stories in Janelle Mon&aacute;e&rsquo;s song lyrics, including her recurring nods to the plot of the 1927 sci-fi film <em>Metropolis</em>.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"34b2776c-895c-47f3-b64a-c400dfb4cf82","content":"<p>appreciate the</p>"},{"id":"45ce0934-541f-4220-9bab-32a7c7b3eb9f","content":"<p>appreciate. The</p>"},{"id":"f40b39f9-1530-4576-91ed-91e5190c5ba7","content":"<p>appreciate, the</p>"},{"id":"0797b545-52b3-4cb5-be9d-077907916f19","content":"<p>appreciate: the</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["34b2776c-895c-47f3-b64a-c400dfb4cf82"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested here is punctuation between a verb and object. No punctuation is needed between the verb ("appreciate") and its object ("the multiple references..."). The object helps complete the idea of the verb&mdash;in this case, it explains what fans of science fiction will appreciate&mdash;and any punctuation between the two results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-719-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$599fcf4d-2e56-4728-8f38-6ba924b930d4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"96499989","external_id":"0e57e42b-00aa-455d-8210-140c29759d6e","vaultid":"899660e0-ee21-4503-93a1-6fce4962bd70","uId":"ae6c4a4f-1573-402c-873e-2bd0b1f20018","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Fans of science fiction will <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> multiple references to classic sci-fi stories in Janelle Mon&aacute;e&rsquo;s song lyrics, including her recurring nods to the plot of the 1927 sci-fi film <em>Metropolis</em>.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"34b2776c-895c-47f3-b64a-c400dfb4cf82","content":"<p>appreciate the</p>"},{"id":"45ce0934-541f-4220-9bab-32a7c7b3eb9f","content":"<p>appreciate. The</p>"},{"id":"f40b39f9-1530-4576-91ed-91e5190c5ba7","content":"<p>appreciate, the</p>"},{"id":"0797b545-52b3-4cb5-be9d-077907916f19","content":"<p>appreciate: the</p>"}],"keys":["34b2776c-895c-47f3-b64a-c400dfb4cf82"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested here is punctuation between a verb and object. No punctuation is needed between the verb (\"appreciate\") and its object (\"the multiple references...\"). The object helps complete the idea of the verb&mdash;in this case, it explains what fans of science fiction will appreciate&mdash;and any punctuation between the two results in an ungrammatical sentence.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the verb and its object.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-719-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"599fcf4d-2e56-4728-8f38-6ba924b930d4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959348,"updateDate":1691007959348}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959348, 1691007959348),
    ($SATQ$96953201$SATQ$, $SATQ$21b4b72d-63c7-49b2-a0cc-f6a157bd755c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$8b3ffccf-e0dd-4546-8c85-08a5a3cc814b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In her two major series &ldquo;Memory Test&rdquo; and &ldquo;Autobiography,&rdquo; painter Howardena Pindell explored themes <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> healing, self-discovery, and memory by cutting and sewing back together pieces of canvas and inserting personal artifacts, such as postcards, into some of the paintings.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"13ff2c75-e680-4e7e-8331-26de021db851","content":"<p>of</p>"},{"id":"e00361d9-dad1-4c79-9e5b-a20c5ddd8c4e","content":"<p>of,</p>"},{"id":"1197b5eb-5fe5-4ae2-8e00-96db68395ea1","content":"<p>of&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"201c69cb-2e71-4422-bfbb-579d7e9ba6cd","content":"<p>of:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["13ff2c75-e680-4e7e-8331-26de021db851"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a preposition and its complement. No punctuation is needed between the preposition &ldquo;of&rdquo; and its complement, the noun phrase &ldquo;healing, self-discovery, and memory.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between a preposition and its complement.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between a preposition and its complement. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between a preposition and its complement.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-702-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$77c1b64a-b511-42d5-b67d-4795b139ecf2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"96953201","external_id":"21b4b72d-63c7-49b2-a0cc-f6a157bd755c","vaultid":"8b3ffccf-e0dd-4546-8c85-08a5a3cc814b","uId":"5229b60c-55ed-4b35-8d63-13fc2467c3ab","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In her two major series &ldquo;Memory Test&rdquo; and &ldquo;Autobiography,&rdquo; painter Howardena Pindell explored themes <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> healing, self-discovery, and memory by cutting and sewing back together pieces of canvas and inserting personal artifacts, such as postcards, into some of the paintings.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"13ff2c75-e680-4e7e-8331-26de021db851","content":"<p>of</p>"},{"id":"e00361d9-dad1-4c79-9e5b-a20c5ddd8c4e","content":"<p>of,</p>"},{"id":"1197b5eb-5fe5-4ae2-8e00-96db68395ea1","content":"<p>of&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"201c69cb-2e71-4422-bfbb-579d7e9ba6cd","content":"<p>of:</p>"}],"keys":["13ff2c75-e680-4e7e-8331-26de021db851"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a preposition and its complement. No punctuation is needed between the preposition &ldquo;of&rdquo; and its complement, the noun phrase &ldquo;healing, self-discovery, and memory.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between a preposition and its complement.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between a preposition and its complement. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between a preposition and its complement.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-702-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"77c1b64a-b511-42d5-b67d-4795b139ecf2","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$96a904c5$SATQ$, $SATQ$c2aa5ede-8913-4159-b5a7-ffb934ed7645$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The musical scores of Japanese composer Hiroyuki Sawano are famous for their mysterious titles. Laden with emojis and seemingly meaningless words, and driven largely by Sawano&rsquo;s &ldquo;personal feeling and mood,&rdquo; <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"8ab49943-87da-4eb4-9e72-c78ce3c40211","content":"<p>the listener can approach each piece free from expectations because of the titles.</p>"},{"id":"a75feccb-195a-433c-8c5e-d7f2b6a89706","content":"<p>the titles allow the listener to approach each piece free from expectations.</p>"},{"id":"ec0b419b-c3a9-4485-ac43-940de79c6309","content":"<p>each piece can be approached by the listener free from expectations because of the titles.</p>"},{"id":"1bf22edd-5463-44f7-8628-d2ff1465cf7f","content":"<p>the listener&rsquo;s approach to each piece because of the titles can be free from expectations.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a75feccb-195a-433c-8c5e-d7f2b6a89706"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;the titles&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrases &ldquo;laden with emojis and seemingly meaningless words, and driven largely by Sawano&rsquo;s &lsquo;personal feeling and mood.&rsquo;&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the titles&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are being described as laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;the listener&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrases illogically suggests that the listener, rather than the titles, is laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;each piece&rdquo; (i.e., musical score) immediately after the modifying phrases illogically suggests that the pieces, rather than the titles of the pieces, are laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;the listener&rsquo;s approach&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrases illogically suggests that the listener&rsquo;s approach, rather than the titles, is laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"96a904c5","external_id":"c2aa5ede-8913-4159-b5a7-ffb934ed7645","vaultid":null,"uId":"c60a5d80-2196-4492-914f-6312107c73cc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The musical scores of Japanese composer Hiroyuki Sawano are famous for their mysterious titles. Laden with emojis and seemingly meaningless words, and driven largely by Sawano&rsquo;s &ldquo;personal feeling and mood,&rdquo; <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"8ab49943-87da-4eb4-9e72-c78ce3c40211","content":"<p>the listener can approach each piece free from expectations because of the titles.</p>"},{"id":"a75feccb-195a-433c-8c5e-d7f2b6a89706","content":"<p>the titles allow the listener to approach each piece free from expectations.</p>"},{"id":"ec0b419b-c3a9-4485-ac43-940de79c6309","content":"<p>each piece can be approached by the listener free from expectations because of the titles.</p>"},{"id":"1bf22edd-5463-44f7-8628-d2ff1465cf7f","content":"<p>the listener&rsquo;s approach to each piece because of the titles can be free from expectations.</p>"}],"keys":["a75feccb-195a-433c-8c5e-d7f2b6a89706"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase &ldquo;the titles&rdquo; the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrases &ldquo;laden with emojis and seemingly meaningless words, and driven largely by Sawano&rsquo;s &lsquo;personal feeling and mood.&rsquo;&rdquo; In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the titles&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;are being described as laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;the listener&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrases illogically suggests that the listener, rather than the titles, is laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;each piece&rdquo; (i.e., musical score) immediately after the modifying phrases illogically suggests that the pieces, rather than the titles of the pieces, are laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of &ldquo;the listener&rsquo;s approach&rdquo; immediately after the modifying phrases illogically suggests that the listener&rsquo;s approach, rather than the titles, is laden with emojis and driven by Sawano&rsquo;s personal feeling.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170)
